 summary: [
  ["Life processes", "Nutrition, respiration, circulation, excretion and reproduction are life processes, essential for the survival of all living beings."],
  ["The alimentary canal", "Food is digested in a long tube from mouth to anus: mouth → oesophagus → stomach → small intestine → large intestine → rectum → anus. The liver and pancreas are associated parts that pour juices into it."],
  ["Mouth and stomach", "Teeth break food into fine pieces (mechanical digestion). Saliva moistens food and breaks starch into sugar, which is why the iodine test shows less starch in chewed rice. The stomach churns food with digestive juice (proteins), acid (kills bacteria) and mucus (protects the lining)."],
  ["Small intestine", "About 6 m long, the longest part. Bile from the liver neutralises acid and breaks fats into droplets; pancreatic juice and intestinal juice complete digestion. Finger-like projections increase the surface area so nutrients are absorbed into the blood."],
  ["Large intestine and egestion", "The large intestine (about 1.5 m, but wider) absorbs water and some salts, forming stool, which is stored in the rectum and removed through the anus (egestion). Fibre and fermented foods keep it healthy."],
  ["Digestion in other animals", "Ruminants like cows bring partly digested food back from the rumen to chew it again (rumination). Birds have no teeth; the gizzard grinds food with the help of grit."],
  ["Breathing", "Air travels nostrils → nasal passages → windpipe → lungs → alveoli. Inhaling: ribs move up and out, diaphragm moves down, chest space increases. Exhaling: the reverse. Exhaled air turns lime water milky because it has more carbon dioxide (about 4–5%) than inhaled air (about 0.04%)."],
  ["Respiration and circulation", "In the alveoli, oxygen enters the blood and carbon dioxide leaves it. Respiration: glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water + energy. Breathing is physical; respiration is chemical. The heart, blood and blood vessels (circulatory system) carry oxygen and nutrients to all parts and remove wastes."],
  ["Breathing in other animals", "Many land animals have lungs; fish breathe with gills; tadpoles use gills, adult frogs use lungs on land and skin in water; earthworms use their moist skin — each suited to its habitat."]
 ]
